# Copy to .env.local.
#
# IN DEVELOPMENT the app runs without most of these (demo-data fallback), so you
# can see the whole UI before standing up a database.
#
# IN PRODUCTION it does NOT. The demo fallback is hard-disabled when
# NODE_ENV=production (no fabricated tutors can ever reach a real user), and the
# app REFUSES TO BOOT without AUTH_SECRET. DATABASE_URL is required.

# --- Postgres (server-side only; when set, the app uses the real DB) ---
# No Docker needed — point this at EITHER:
#   • your existing local Postgres:  postgresql://postgres:YOUR_PASSWORD@localhost:5432/tnajem  (CREATE DATABASE tnajem; first)
#   • a free cloud DB (Neon):        paste the connection string from your Neon project
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/tnajem

# --- Postgres tuning (optional — the defaults are correct for a single VPS) ---
# Read by lib/db/index.ts. See DEPLOY.md §3 and SCALABILITY.md §2.
#
# DB_POOL_MAX — pool size PER NODE PROCESS (default 10). What Postgres actually
#   sees is (pm2 workers × DB_POOL_MAX). Stock Postgres allows 100 connections,
#   minus 3 superuser-reserved, minus ~10 for psql/backups/the purge cron → keep
#   the product UNDER ~87. 1 worker = 10; the recommended 2 workers = 20. If you
#   add workers, LOWER this. A bigger pool is not how you scale (PgBouncer is).
# DB_POOL_MAX=10
#
# DB_PREPARE — server-side prepared statements (default ON; only "0" disables).
#   A real win on the hot point-lookups (sessions.token, tutors.slug). Set to 0 in
#   the SAME change that puts PgBouncer in transaction-pooling mode in front of
#   Postgres — a prepared statement lives on a server connection the next
#   transaction may not get back. Not before.
# DB_PREPARE=1
#
# DB_SSL — auto-detected: TLS required for a REMOTE database, skipped over the
#   loopback (a default local Postgres does not offer TLS at all). Only set this
#   if you move Postgres off the box. Values: require | 0
# DB_SSL=require

# --- Public site URL (canonical origin) ---
# Used by metadataBase, app/robots.ts and the sitemap. Defaults to https://tnajem.tn
# when unset. Set it per-environment (e.g. a preview/staging URL) so preview
# deploys don't emit canonical links + a sitemap pointing at production.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://tnajem.tn

# --- Auth (sessions + phone OTP) ---
# Secret every OTP hash is derived from: sha256(`phone:code:AUTH_SECRET`).
#
# REQUIRED AT RUNTIME IN PRODUCTION — with NODE_ENV=production and this unset,
# lib/auth.ts logs a FATAL CONFIG line on boot and then THROWS on the first OTP
# hash, so every login fails loudly. That is deliberate: the dev default below is
# public in this repo, so with it in place an attacker can precompute the hash of
# all 1,000,000 codes for a phone offline, and the 5-attempt budget stops meaning
# anything. A silent fallback would ship guessable-hash auth and nobody would find out.
#
# NOTE: it is resolved LAZILY (on first use), NOT at module load — so it is NOT
# needed to run `next build`. Do not "fix" that by throwing at import time: every
# page's server bundle imports lib/auth (SiteShell → SiteHeader → app/actions), so
# a module-load throw kills the build on any box that injects secrets at runtime.
#
# Generate a real one: openssl rand -hex 32
AUTH_SECRET=dev-insecure-secret-change-me
# --- OTP channel ---
# Where a login code is sent. "email" (default) or "sms".
#
# This is the ENTIRE revert path. The SMS implementation is still live and
# compiling in lib/sms.ts and app/actions.ts — it was not commented out, because
# commented code is invisible to tsc and to the audit gates and rots the moment
# anything around it moves. Set this to "sms" and restart to go back to phone
# login; no rebuild, no code change.
#
# Note it also selects the ADMIN allowlist below (ADMIN_EMAILS vs ADMIN_PHONES),
# because the two channels store their identity in different columns.
OTP_CHANNEL=email

# --- Email (OTP delivery over SMTP, via nodemailer) ---
# Login codes are emailed only when HOST + USER + PASS + FROM_ADDRESS are all set;
# otherwise the code is shown on-screen (dev mode only — never once a provider is
# configured, see requestOtp). Swap providers by changing these values, not code.
#
# GMAIL NOTES, learned the hard way:
#  • MAIL_PASS must be an APP PASSWORD (Google Account → Security → App passwords),
#    not the account password. 2FA has to be on for that menu to exist.
#  • MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS should EQUAL MAIL_USER. Gmail rewrites the From header to
#    the authenticated account unless the address is a verified alias, so a
#    mismatch silently ships mail that says something other than what you set.
#  • A free gmail.com account is capped around 500 recipients/day (Workspace:
#    2000). That is a real ceiling for a launch expecting signups — every login
#    attempt spends one. Move to a transactional provider before it bites.
#  • Port 465 = implicit TLS (MAIL_SECURE=true). Port 587 = STARTTLS (false).
#    Leave MAIL_SECURE unset and lib/mail.ts infers it from the port.
# MAIL_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# MAIL_PORT=465
# MAIL_SECURE=true
# MAIL_USER=support@example.com
# MAIL_PASS=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# MAIL_FROM_NAME=Tnajem
# MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=support@example.com
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=support@example.com

# --- SMS (the OTP_CHANNEL=sms path; via Twilio REST — no SDK needed) ---
# Also the optional second channel for notify(): booking confirmations and
# verification decisions are texted when a profile has a phone on file. Since
# email became the login identity, that number is collected during onboarding
# (/student/welcome, /onboarding) and is optional — so this stays useful even
# while OTP_CHANNEL=email.
# Login codes are texted
# only when SID + TOKEN + (FROM *or* MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID) are all set;
# otherwise the code is shown on-screen (dev mode only — never in production).
# For Tunisia, a Messaging Service with a registered alphanumeric sender ID
# usually delivers better than a US long code. Set ONE of FROM / MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID.
# Swap for a Tunisian gateway by editing lib/sms.ts (provider-agnostic).
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_FROM=+1XXXXXXXXXX                 # a Twilio number (delivers to +216), OR:
# TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=MGxxxxxxxx  # recommended: Messaging Service w/ sender ID

# --- Admin (tutor verification review) ---
# Who may open /admin/verifications and approve/reject tutors. Empty = NOBODY is
# admin — the gate fails closed, deliberately: that queue holds every uploaded
# national ID scan.
#
# Which of these two is read follows OTP_CHANNEL, because that decides which
# column a profile's identity lives in. Set the one that matches.
# Comma-separated, same format as the login identity.
ADMIN_EMAILS=
ADMIN_PHONES=

# --- Storage (uploaded verification documents — SENSITIVE) ---
# Tutors upload national ID scans here. Under Tunisia's INPDP regime (Loi 2004-63)
# these are sensitive personal data: keep them off the public web root and out of git.
#
# Dev: defaults to a local ./.storage folder (gitignored).
# PROD: MUST be a PERSISTENT VOLUME (e.g. a Render disk mounted at /var/data).
#   On an ephemeral filesystem the ID docs vanish on every redeploy, which both
#   breaks the admin review queue and leaves orphaned verification_docs rows
#   pointing at files that no longer exist. Back this folder up with Postgres.
# STORAGE_DIR=/var/data

# --- Retention purge (see lib/retention.ts) ---
# ONE daily job, TWO things purged:
#   1. ID documents past the 90-day window. /privacy promises identity documents
#      are deleted at most 90 days after the verification decision — nothing
#      enforces that unless the purge actually runs.
#   2. Expired sessions + otp_codes. Both tables grow by one row per login / per
#      OTP request, forever; nothing else ever deletes them.
#
# Run it EITHER as a cron'd CLI ....... npm run db:purge          (add --dry-run to preview)
#                 OR as an HTTP cron ... POST /api/cron/purge with an
#                                        "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" header.
#
# CRON_SECRET protects that route. Generate one: openssl rand -hex 32
# If it is unset, /api/cron/purge refuses to run (503) rather than exposing an
# unauthenticated destructive endpoint — so in prod this is REQUIRED.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Payments (money movement — OFF until legal sign-off) ---
# Master switch, read by paymentsEnabled() in lib/payments.ts. ONLY "1" enables it;
# unset/empty = OFF, so an env typo can never start moving money.
# While OFF: every payment adapter method throws, and the tutor balance is a real 0
# (we never fabricate earnings). Do NOT set this to 1 until counsel signs off AND
# the provider contract + webhook signature verification actually exist.
PAYMENTS_ENABLED=

# --- Live video (MVP: a default Jitsi room base, or per-class links) ---
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEFAULT_MEET_BASE=https://meet.jit.si/tnajem-

# --- LATER (gated on legal sign-off — DO NOT wire until counsel confirms) ---
# KONNECT_API_KEY=
# KONNECT_WALLET_ID=
# FLOUCI_APP_TOKEN=
# FLOUCI_APP_SECRET=
# --- LATER (WhatsApp reminders) ---
# WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_ID=

# ── Document store ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STORAGE_DIR is REQUIRED once this is a monorepo, in dev as well as prod.
#
# The dev fallback is `join(process.cwd(), ".storage")`, and cwd now depends on
# WHICH WORKSPACE ran the command: `npm run db:purge` executes inside
# packages/db, `next build` inside apps/web, and Next's standalone server.js
# chdir()s to its own folder. Leave it unset and the purge looks in
# packages/db/.storage while the ID scans sit in apps/web/.storage.
#
# That is not a cosmetic mismatch. The purge treats a missing file as non-fatal:
# it counts the document "already gone" and DELETES THE ROW ANYWAY. A job with
# the wrong base directory therefore deletes every row, orphans every national
# ID scan on disk, and reports success. Set it explicitly, always.
# In production it must be a PERSISTENT volume, and storageBase() throws rather
# than guess.
STORAGE_DIR=/var/lib/tnajem/storage
